{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_735327732#0_1027950557","relevance":3,"reason":"WIRED article is dedicated to China's Great Firewall and state internet censorship, providing direct historical evidence about government control of citizens' access to information online.","learned":"As early as 1997, reporting described Chinese ISPs, internet cafés, and state censorship committees as part of Beijing's effort to control online information."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q580","reason":"This query is yielding dedicated censorship sources; continue to gather more grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q580"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_388051774#0_818229761","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q580","rank":3,"snippet":"The affects of communism on people's lives in the People's Republic of China are not as striking or blatantly nefarious as anti-communist propaganda of the early and mid-20th century would lead you to believe. In many ways, China is becoming increasingly westernized. Explore this article Censorship of Speech and Print What Gets Cut Communism's Toll on Previous Generations How to Determine the Effects of Communism 1 Censorship of Speech and Print ... Chad Baker/Photodisc/Getty Images Though often depicted as a blatant handicap to <cover>media and social interaction, censorship in China is often very subtle and, although it tends to forbid criticism of the government, </cover>is itself governed by no exact rules. Particularly now, in the Age of Information, China is flirting with the boundaries of what information to allow within its borders. Although much of the Internet is still censored or restricted, the government understands that, to play a role in global politics, information needs to be allowed in. The uncertainty about which sorts of information will prompt growth and what might endanger the regime, however, is the basis for what Elizabeth C. Economy, a senior fellow on the Council on Foreign Relations, calls \""}}
