{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ china chinese) (+ \"freedom on the net\" \"freedom house\") (+ censorship surveillance \"internet freedom\") (+ worst last score))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1996725240#2_2152285609","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q582","rank":1,"snippet":"freedom Internet freedom From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia See also References \" , \"segment\": \"This is troublesome because many people today use the Internet for their daily news and weather, as opposed to television. This change has continued throughout the past decade and will continue to increase. It allows for the increased flow of information and allows information to be obtained faster than ever before. Events such as elections and disasters are known about within seconds rather than hours or days. Not everywhere relishes in the luxury though. \\\" The People's Republic of China (PRC) has the world's largest number of Internet users, estimated at 330 million people, including 70 million bloggers. It also has one of the most sophisticated and aggressive Internet censorship and control regimes in the world. In 2020 <cover>Freedom House ranked China last of 64 nations in internet freedom. </cover>See also Pirate Party References ^ Altman, Alex (2008-02-20). \" A Coming Chill Over Internet Freedom?\".\" , \"start_char\": 1369.000000 , \"title\": \"Internet freedom - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_freedom\" }"}}
