{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_71198373#13_146313616","relevance":3,"reason":"Reporters Without Borders' 'China - The Enemies of Internet' page is dedicated to China's internet censorship/surveillance and gives direct evidence about monitoring and interception of TOM-Skype chats and embedded censorship capabilities.","learned":"RSF/OpenNet reported that in China, TOM-Skype could monitor/intercept chats, capture everyday conversations on servers, and trigger storage based on usernames or message participants."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q586","reason":"This targeted RSF/press-freedom query produced a strong dedicated China result; continue to find additional authoritative evidence from the same result set."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q586"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1630624872#10_3627290962","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q586","rank":2,"snippet":"to the outside world matters, the digital links are deteriorating.” Foreign Media “Some people in China don’t look at freedom of speech as an abstract ideal, but more as a means to an end.” U.S. Technology in China U.S. Relations With China Circumventing the Censors Top Stories on CFR \" , \"segment\": \"More on: China Censorship and Freedom of Expression Digital Policy Media Xi Jinping Exerting Control The <cover>Chinese government deploys myriad ways of censoring the internet. The Golden Shield Project, colloquially known as the Great Firewall, is the center of the government’s online censorship and surveillance effort. Its methods include bandwidth throttling, keyword filtering, and blocking access to certain websites. According to Reporters Without Borders, </cover>the firewall makes large-scale use of Deep Packet Inspection technology to block access based on keyword detection. As Ng points out, the government also employs a diverse range of methods to induce journalists to censor themselves, including dismissals and demotions, libel lawsuits, fines, arrests, and forced televised confessions. “To the degree that China’s connection to the outside world matters, the digital links are deteriorating.” Evan Osnos, New Yorker Facebook Twitter LinkedIn Email As of "}}
