{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_1653643239#6_3629307066","relevance":1,"reason":"The Amnesty page is mainly about Hong Kong protests and only briefly mentions that altering music to satisfy censorship in China is normal in the music industry; it is not dedicated evidence about the overall extent of Chinese government censorship of citizens.","learned":"Amnesty-related reporting notes that artists may alter musical work to cater to censorship in China, with chilling effects linked to expression in Hong Kong/mainland contexts."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ china chinese) (+ rsf \"reporters without borders\") (+ censorship propaganda surveillance) (+ \"media control\" \"press freedom\"))","reason":"Seek more authoritative RSF evidence specifically on China's censorship, propaganda, surveillance, and press/media control, which is likely to yield dedicated grade-3 documentation."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ china chinese) (+ rsf \"reporters without borders\") (+ censorship propaganda surveillance) (+ \"media control\" \"press freedom\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1275864088#1_1435060949","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q611","rank":1,"snippet":"flow of information on the Internet. Violence against journalists, netizens, and media assistants, including abuses attributable to the state, armed militias, clandestine organisations or pressure groups, are monitored by RSF staff during the year and are also part of the final score. A smaller score on the report corresponds to greater freedom of the press as reported by the organisation. The questionnaire is sent to Reporters Without Borders's partner organisations: 18 freedom of expression non-governmental organisations located in five continents, its 150 correspondents around the world and journalists, researchers, jurists and human rights activists. In 2021, the countries which ranked highest on the Press Freedom Index were Norway, Finland, Sweden, Denmark, Costa Rica, the Netherlands, Jamaica, New Zealand, Portugal, and Switzerland. The lowest-ranking countries were Eritrea, North Korea, Turkmenistan, <cover>China, Djibouti, Vietnam, Iran, Syria, Laos, and Cuba. See also Censorship by country Internet censorship by country List of freedom indices References ^ \"2021 World Press Freedom Index\". Reporters Without Borders.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 1207.000000 , \"title\": \"Press Freedom Index - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Press_Freedom_Index\" }"}}
